answers.body:
I don't know where you got that struct syntax from. That doesn't look very OpenMP-like but maybe I just don't know that. Anyway, this is how you define a custom reduction in OpenMP-4. #pragma omp declare reduction \
    (complex_double_reduction : std::complex<double> : omp_out += omp_in) \
    initializer(omp_priv = std::complex<double>(0, 0))

int main() {
    const int n = 100;
    std::complex<double> sum(0, 0);

#   pragma omp parallel for reduction(complex_double_reduction:sum)
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        std::complex<double> val(i, -i);
        sum += val;
    }

    std::cout << "Sum: " << sum << std::endl;
    return 0;
} The initializer clause is optional and usually not needed. Not all compilers require a custom reduction operator if the + operator is overloaded for the relevant type. In clang-16 you can just write reduction(+:sum) but this behavior is not portable at the moment.

