Direct answer: I don't know why you see the performance difference. Also: false sharing is irrelevant in your code. False sharing is only a problem if there are many repeated access to the same cacheline from different threads. Indirect answer: modern processors and compilers almost completely do away with false sharing. Let me give an example. Suppose I have an array a with length p where p is the number of threads. And each thread t continually writes in a[t] . That's a textbook example of false sharing, not? The following code (computing pi by Riemann integration) runs twice, once with threads writing at distance 1, meaning false sharing, and once at distance 8 meaning a cacheline apart: long int steps = steps_per_thread*nthreads;
