The private ( i, j, k ) declaration is not optional. Add it back, otherwise the inner loop variables j and k are shared, which completely messes up the inner loops. It is better to declare variables as locally as possible. That makes reasoning about OpenMP code much easier: clock_t begin_time = clock(); 
# pragma omp parallel
{
# pragma omp for
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {
                C[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j];
            }
        }
    }
} 
return float(clock() - begin_time); In that case, A,B,C will be shared by default  - coming from the outside, and j,k are private because they are declared within the parallel scope. The loop variable of a parallel for is always implicitly private.

