answers.body:
Try #pragma omp parallel for private(i)
for (i = 0; i < num_tet_phys; i++)
  tet_phys[i].cal_Mn(...); Note the use of parallel for . and compile with the -fopenmp flag. The #pragma omp parallel creates a team of threads, all of which execute the next statement (in your case, the entire for loop). After the statement, the threads join back into one. The #pragma omp parallel for creates a team of threads, which divide the work of the for loop between them.

